 hiring have more to gain from the  arrangement than the contractors in our  case then it means selling web design  writing or other skills online it can  also mean selling directly to the  clients with services such as personal  training coaching or even gardening  though the term is used less often to  describe a b2c operations like this at  any rate because we're going out and  finding the work rather than relying on  a single company to provide it the  balance of power is shifted in our favor  and we get to control our time and  income you can use the gig economy as  your full-time career choice and enjoy  the freedom that come  from picking job you light and skipping  those you don't you can also get  unconventional this way with how you  divide your Worth and time off for  instance I only gig four days a week  which leaves me with one day to work on  the Pioneer books and other projects but  it can also be used in many other ways  for instance you can dig part-time and  take on a part-time job or you can gig a  little in the evenings in order to bring  in extra cash if you have kids and one  of you is forced to take extended leave  to look after them digging online could  be a great way to make up for that lost  income while being a full-time mom or  dad of course it's ideal if you're  someone who enjoys traveling and you  want to work on the road this is what a  digital Nomad is gigging can be used to  raise funds to pursue other dream jobs  or projects or to fill out your  portfolio and CV the key then is to  identify what you're good at what skills  you have and then in what way you're  going to sell those skills in order to  support the kind of lifestyle you want  this is our aim and I go into a lot more  depth regarding lifestyle design in the  book packaging your services so start  with the lifestyle you want or your end  goals and then design your getting  around to that know how much you need to  earn what hours you want to work whether  you need to be able to work on the road  and whether you'd rather have lots of  clients or just a few steady ones now  decide on what you're going to offer and  package that service in the way that  suits you you can even offer multiple  packages with different pricing tiers if  you're selling writing services for  instance is up to you with you charge  per word per 500 words a blog post per  hour  or for a set of blog posts ordered on a  recurring monthly basis perhaps maybe  you want to sell website starter packs  with 200 blog posts at a discounted rate  each has its pros and cons and each will  suit different lifestyles try to agree  how many revisions are going to offer at  that price how much communication is  included whether you provide formatting  or perhaps whether this incurs of  slightly steeper fee the more you  outline in your offering the less  confusion there will be and the more you  can fine-tune your own workflow process  fixes in the book I talked about  increasing focus and for activity by  creating the right working environment  and even using flow hacks I also discuss  using technology in software as force  multipliers to help you produce more  work with the same amount of effort but  one of my favorite strategies comes from  my wife's work the process fix  that means looking at any routine  process that you're using your workflow  and finding ways to make it more  efficient for instance I used to write  blog posts and then proofread them and  upload them to WordPress then I'd add  the formatting and the images now though  I instead do the proofread at the same  time as the formatting and adding the  images essentially removing one of the  steps and thereby saving myself up to 10  to 20 minutes per blog post seeing as  I'm getting paid per job that represents  a rather huge increase in my overall  salary and this is what's really  interesting here at the gig economy  thereby becoming more efficient yourself  you can increase your wages without  actually changing the amount that you're  charging anyone in the book I also  talked about how to negotiate higher  rates even a tiny increase per word can  translate to huge pay rises that you'd  never be able to accomplish in  traditional employment look at each  stage in your workflow in-depth and ask  if it could be made more efficient  communication overhead one way that  almost any business can be made more  efficient is to cut down on  communication overhead this is a term I  got from the personal MBA by Josh  Kaufman it means the amount of time and  therefore money that is wasted on  unnecessary emails and Skype calls I  advise avoiding clients that are intent  on having long Skype meetings that  discuss small orders and trying to keep  email chatter to a minimum wherever  possible in fact I actually personally  choose to use agents and intermediaries  to cut down on this first they take a  cut of my profits but he also fined me  the work and handle all complaints as  well as negotiations etc that means I  can just focus on work and avoid wasting  my mental energy despite giving away  some of my profits per job I ultimately  earn more by being a complete more work  go to market strategy the go-to-market  strategy simply means finding where  people who want your services are likely  to congregate from web design  copywriting video editing coding and  more it's common to find work from sites  like up work and freelancer problem is  that you're competing with a lot of  other professionals here I'm going to be  forced to work for that websites  guidelines often to your detriment  so I'm actually encourage you to record  your screen for instance so that clients  can see exactly what you're doing  throughout your day which is highly  invasive so ask instead where do the  people you're selling to hang out  what do they read it could be a  subreddit it could be a forum it could  be an industry magazine maybe it means  working with an influencer in that niche  and getting them to give you a shout out  I sell most of my services via webmaster  forums and via agencies this is free  it's simple and it's provided me with  steady work now for almost 18 years I  give plenty more tips and where you can  find work of all kinds in the book know  your worth building a basic website  using WordPress is an incredibly easy  task that will take about a day for  anyone with even moderate tech skills  nevertheless big companies pay thousands  of dollars of this work because they  don't know any different if you try and  sell them a web design for Less don't  presume you're offering an inferior  product and they'll likely pass you over  to choose the more expensive option that  normally means a big company or agency  that isn't quite as efficient as you are  because you're so much more lean point  is that it doesn't matter if your skill  seems easy it's the knowledge gap and  the value that they get out of it that  should determine what you're charging so  make sure you know your Worth and don't  be afraid to ask for it become a  superstar  that said if you do try and sell your  skills to a big corporation you're  probably still going to be passed up in  favor of more established and stuffy  business thus you'll be forced to go  after the small fry and compete with  smaller individuals instead until your  business plan is ridiculous  so as an individual what can you do to  fix the situation and compete with  bigger companies the answer is to become  an online superstar and to build your  own personal brand you do this by taking  on high-profile jobs to build an amazing  portfolio you do it by filling out your  CV by continuing to learn and train  yourself you can also create a blog or a  blog we discuss your skills try to  imagine the procurement department at  the company hiring your work discussing  your merits versus the web design  company or programming agency that  you're competing with if you're just  someone they found on upwork then you're  not going to compete with the  professional outfit but if you've worked  on projects for Google and Facebook if  you've built your own highly successful  app if you've authored books and if  you've got your own coding blog with an  audience of hundreds of thousands  suddenly you're a contender and you can  charge the really big bucks so if you  found any of these tips helpful or  interesting then you should get a lot of  value out of the book there I go into  much more depth regarding how to set up  a business after transition safely from  regular work and how to build a  resilient  that provides stable and growing income  I also go into some depth regarding the  market forces that gave birth to the gig  economy and how it's likely to evolve  and grow in exciting ways in the future  the short version this type of work is  becoming increasingly common and so by  figuring out your place in this gig  economy now you can gain the upper hand  on the competition and lift the  lifestyle that you really want  so thanks ton for watching guys hope you  found this video useful and interesting  if you did then of course check out the  book but also subscribe to the channel  where you'll find much more on building  a business on productivity on  self-improvement also fitness brain  training all that usual stuff if that  sounds good  then thanks a ton for watching stay  tuned and bye for now
 

